From: mx-kernel
Subject: [PATCH] Poll the server loop with a timeout; add an idle callback

xserver_zmq_default::start_impl calls poll_channels(-1), which blocks until a
message arrives. That has two consequences for an embedded kernel.

1. stop() cannot take effect. stop_impl() sets a flag, but the loop only
   re-tests it after poll_channels returns, and poll_channels does not return
   until a client sends something. If nothing arrives -- the normal case after
   a shutdown -- the loop never exits, never reaches stop_channels(), and the
   publisher and heartbeat threads never receive their stop message. The
   xserver_zmq_impl destructor then blocks forever joining them:

       xserver_zmq_impl::~xserver_zmq_impl()
         xthread::~xthread() -> join() -> __ulock_wait   [blocked]

   For a kernel embedded in a host application, that is a hang of the host on
   quit. The only workaround from outside the library is to leak the kernel and
   context deliberately, which is what this project did before this patch.

2. Nothing can be published while the kernel is idle. The IOPub socket is owned
   by this thread, ZMQ sockets are not thread-safe, and this thread is parked
   in poll(). An embedder that wants to emit output not triggered by an
   execute_request has nowhere to do it safely.

This patch polls with a configurable timeout (default 100ms) and invokes an
optional callback on each tick where no message arrived. That fixes both: the
loop observes is_stopped() promptly and shuts the channels down properly, and
the callback runs on the thread that owns the sockets, so an embedder can
publish from it.

Setting a negative timeout restores the previous blocking behaviour.

Also makes xserver_zmq_impl::m_request_stop atomic. It is written by the thread
calling stop() and read by the server thread. That was already a data race, but
a benign-looking one while the loop was parked in poll(); once the loop reads
the flag on every tick the compiler is free to hoist the load out of the loop,
and a stop request can be missed entirely.

Verified by integration tests in
source/projects/kernel/tests/test_server_shutdown.cpp, which start a real
kernel on loopback and assert that stop() is observed, that the thread joins,
and that the destructor completes. Reverting the poll change to -1 makes those
tests hang, which their watchdog reports as a failure.

Applies to: xeus-zmq 3.1.1
Upstream: not yet reported -- see patches/README.md

diff -ru a/include/xeus-zmq/xserver_zmq.hpp b/include/xeus-zmq/xserver_zmq.hpp
--- a/include/xeus-zmq/xserver_zmq.hpp
+++ b/include/xeus-zmq/xserver_zmq.hpp
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #ifndef XEUS_SERVER_ZMQ_HPP
 #define XEUS_SERVER_ZMQ_HPP
 
+#include <functional>
 #include <optional>
 
 #include "xeus/xeus_context.hpp"
@@ -30,8 +31,31 @@
 
         using xserver::notify_internal_listener;
 
+        // LOCAL PATCH (mx-kernel) -- idle callback and configurable poll
+        // timeout. See patches/README.md.
+        //
+        // An embedded kernel needs two things the blocking poll cannot give:
+        // a chance to notice that stop() was requested, and a chance to do
+        // work on the thread that owns the ZMQ sockets (publishing on IOPub
+        // from any other thread is a data race). Polling with a timeout and
+        // invoking a callback on each idle tick provides both.
+        //
+        // Both setters must be called before start(); they are not
+        // synchronised, and the server thread reads them once it is running.
+        using idle_callback_type = std::function<void()>;
+        void set_idle_callback(idle_callback_type cb);
+
+        // Poll timeout in milliseconds. A negative value blocks indefinitely,
+        // which restores the historical behaviour but means stop() is not
+        // observed until the next message arrives.
+        void set_poll_timeout(long timeout_ms);
+        long get_poll_timeout() const;
+
     protected:
 
+        // Invoked by inheriting classes when a poll times out with no message.
+        void notify_idle();
+
         xserver_zmq(xcontext& context,
                     const xconfiguration& config,
                     nl::json::error_handler_t eh);
@@ -64,6 +88,10 @@
         void update_config_impl(xconfiguration& config) const override;
 
         std::unique_ptr<xserver_zmq_impl> p_impl;
+
+        // LOCAL PATCH (mx-kernel)
+        idle_callback_type m_idle_callback;
+        long m_poll_timeout = 100;
     };
 
     XEUS_ZMQ_API
diff -ru a/src/server/xserver_zmq.cpp b/src/server/xserver_zmq.cpp
--- a/src/server/xserver_zmq.cpp
+++ b/src/server/xserver_zmq.cpp
@@ -31,6 +31,30 @@
     // API for inheriting classes //
     ////////////////////////////////
 
+    // LOCAL PATCH (mx-kernel) -- see patches/README.md
+    void xserver_zmq::set_idle_callback(idle_callback_type cb)
+    {
+        m_idle_callback = std::move(cb);
+    }
+
+    void xserver_zmq::set_poll_timeout(long timeout_ms)
+    {
+        m_poll_timeout = timeout_ms;
+    }
+
+    long xserver_zmq::get_poll_timeout() const
+    {
+        return m_poll_timeout;
+    }
+
+    void xserver_zmq::notify_idle()
+    {
+        if (m_idle_callback)
+        {
+            m_idle_callback();
+        }
+    }
+
     void xserver_zmq::start_publisher_thread()
     {
         p_impl->start_publisher_thread();
diff -ru a/src/server/xserver_zmq_default.cpp b/src/server/xserver_zmq_default.cpp
--- a/src/server/xserver_zmq_default.cpp
+++ b/src/server/xserver_zmq_default.cpp
@@ -25,9 +25,17 @@
 
         publish(std::move(msg), channel::SHELL);
 
+        // LOCAL PATCH (mx-kernel) -- poll with a timeout instead of blocking
+        // forever. See patches/README.md.
+        //
+        // With poll_channels(-1) this loop parks until a message arrives, so
+        // after stop() it never re-tests is_stopped(), never reaches
+        // stop_channels() below, and the destructor then blocks forever
+        // joining the publisher and heartbeat threads. Polling lets the loop
+        // exit, and gives an embedder a hook to run work on this thread.
         while(!is_stopped())
         {
-            auto msg = poll_channels(-1);
+            auto msg = poll_channels(get_poll_timeout());
             if (msg)
             {
                 if (msg.value().second == channel::SHELL)
@@ -38,6 +46,10 @@
                 {
                     notify_control_listener(std::move(msg.value().first));
                 }
+            }
+            else
+            {
+                notify_idle();
             }
         }
 
diff -ru a/src/server/xserver_zmq_impl.hpp b/src/server/xserver_zmq_impl.hpp
--- a/src/server/xserver_zmq_impl.hpp
+++ b/src/server/xserver_zmq_impl.hpp
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #ifndef XEUS_SERVER_ZMQ_IMPL_HPP
 #define XEUS_SERVER_ZMQ_IMPL_HPP
 
+#include <atomic>
 #include <memory>
 
 #include "zmq.hpp"
@@ -85,7 +86,12 @@
 
         nl::json::error_handler_t m_error_handler;
 
-        bool m_request_stop;
+        // LOCAL PATCH (mx-kernel) -- atomic, not plain bool.
+        // The server loop polls with a timeout and reads this every tick,
+        // while stop() writes it from another thread. As a plain bool that is
+        // a data race, and the compiler is free to hoist the read out of the
+        // loop, so a stop request could be missed entirely.
+        std::atomic<bool> m_request_stop;
     };
 }
 
